<section>
    <h3>Redacting without damaging the response</h3>
    <pre style="font-size: 0.45em;"><code class="language-json" data-trim>
{"meta":{},"password":"x","n":1.0,"big":12345678901234567890}
    </code></pre>
    <div class="fragment">
        <p class="label-before">json_decode + json_encode</p>
        <pre style="font-size: 0.45em;"><code class="language-json" data-trim>
{"meta":[],"password":"[REDACTED]","n":1,"big":1.2345678901234567e+19}
        </code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="fragment">
        <p class="label-after">In-place replacement</p>
        <pre style="font-size: 0.45em;"><code class="language-json" data-trim>
{"meta":{},"password":"[REDACTED]","n":1.0,"big":12345678901234567890}
        </code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="fragment callout">
        The JSON is no longer decoded then re-encoded: sensitive values are replaced in a single pass,
        the rest is <b>byte-for-byte identical</b> (<code>{}</code>, floats, big integers, escapes, formatting).
    </p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>A subtler point. Here's a small JSON: an empty meta object, a password, a float 1.0, and a very big integer.</p>
        <p>[click] The naive approach to redact the password is json_decode, replace the value, then json_encode. And look at the result: the empty object became an empty array. The 1.0 became 1. And the big integer became a float in scientific notation.</p>
        <p>[click] The chosen solution: an in-place replacement. Only the value of the password changes.</p>
        <p>[click] The JSON is no longer decoded then re-encoded: sensitive values are replaced in a single pass, and everything else is byte-for-byte identical. Empty objects, floats, big integers, escapes, and even the formatting.</p>
        <p>Why does it matter so much? Imagine an assertSame on the response body. In record mode, the test gets the real response: it passes. In replay mode, it gets the re-encoded version: it fails. A test that passes when recording and fails when replaying is the kind of bug that costs you an afternoon.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
